  The last line adds four conjunction nodes and the outer existential: its binder and the seven-node bound $n^(j^(1 + 1))$ contribute nine nodes with the quantifier itself. Renaming the expanded binders consecutively as $v_100 \, dots.h \, v_127$ avoids all capture; the bound of each quantifier is renamed in the preceding environment. Only $v_1 \, v_2 \, v_3$ remain free. Every node then costs at most $128$, so $Q$ costs at most $128 dot.op 1394 = 178432$. Outside the two numeral assignments, the rest of the program costs $29$: initialization costs $6$, the while and negation nodes cost $2$, increment costs $11$, final output costs $6$, and the four sequencing nodes cost $4$. In particular the following much larger allowance bounds the entire program:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(17.17)")
   $ 100 \( n + 1 \)^3 + \( 2 dot.op 10^6 \)^204 < 2^(\( M_(*) + 10 \)^4) = k . $ <q:program-size>]
  The second summand exceeds $178461$. For the strict inequality, $M_(*) + 1 lt.eq 2^(M_(*))$ bounds the first summand by $2^(3 M_(*) + 7)$, and $2 dot.op 10^6 < 2^21$ bounds the second by $2^4284$. Since $M_(*) > 10^9$, their sum is less than $2^(3 M_(*) + 8)$, which is less than $2^(\( M_(*) + 10 \)^4)$. ◻
